<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Okavango Delta</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Okavango Swamps</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Okavango Delta is a vast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-niger-delta">inland delta</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>, where the Okavango River spreads out into channels, lagoons and islands and evaporates into the Kalahari without reaching the sea. Its seasonal floods support abundant wildlife. It has been home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wayeyi">Wayeyi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hambukushu">Hambukushu</a> and Batawana, and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2014.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/okavango-delta.jpg" alt="Okavango Delta" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joachim Huber · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Okavango_Delta,_Botswana1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Okavango Delta lies in the central part of the endorheic basin of the Kalahari Desert, where the Okavango River reaches a tectonic trough at an elevation of 930 to 1,000 metres. The river is formed by the Cubango and Cuito rivers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and enters the delta near Mohembo, where it divides into channels, floodplains, lagoons and permanent swamps. Nearly all the water reaching the delta is lost to evaporation and transpiration or seeps into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquifer">aquifer</a> below, while a small part leaves by the Thamalakane and Boteti rivers.</p><p>The delta is very flat, varying less than 2 metres in height across about 15,000 square kilometres. Rain falling on the Angolan highlands in January and February travels some 1,200 km in about a month and spreads over the delta between March and June, so that flood levels peak from June to August, during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>'s dry winter. About 70 percent of the thousands of islands began as termite mounds. Chief's Island, the largest, was historically reserved as a hunting area for the chief.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Okavango Delta peoples consist of five groups: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hambukushu">Hambukushu</a>, the Dceriku, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wayeyi">Wayeyi</a>, the Bugakhwe and the ǁanikhwe. The first three have traditionally combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a> farming, fishing, hunting, gathering and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastoralism">pastoralism</a>, while the last two, who are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-people">Bushmen</a>, have lived by fishing, hunting and gathering. The delta has been under the political control of the Batawana, a Tswana nation, since the late 18th century. Led by the house of Mathiba I, they established complete control in the 1850s as the regional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> trade expanded. Most Batawana lived at the edges of the delta because the tsetse fly threatened their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, though many lived in the swamps from 1896 to the late 1930s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The delta is home to all of the big five game animals, over 400 bird species, and 71 species of fish; the red lechwe is its most abundant large mammal. Its flora includes 1,068 plants, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> growing in the deeper channels. The Moremi Game Reserve lies on its eastern side. On 22 June 2014 the delta became the 1,000th site inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List, as one of the few interior delta systems without an outlet to the sea.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Okavango Delta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They eventually settled near Lake Ngami at the edge of the <b>Okavango</b> delta, where they established authority over the BaYei, Hambukushu and other local peoples.</blockquote><a href="/state/batawana-chiefdom">Batawana Chiefdom</a></li></ul></section>
